The landing page's layout: <code><nowiki>{{home}}</nowiki></code> loads the stylesheet, and
<code><nowiki>{{home/route|Installation}}</nowiki></code> is one of the buttons that start a reader off.

== Why a template at all ==

<code><nowiki>{{home}}</nowiki></code> takes no arguments and renders nothing. It exists so the
stylesheet's name is written down once, in the same shape as
<code><nowiki>{{prevnext}}</nowiki></code>, rather than in a bare
<code><nowiki><templatestyles /></nowiki></code> on the page itself.

<code><nowiki>{{home/route}}</nowiki></code> takes a page name, and a second argument of any
value makes that card the filled one. The label is the target's own translated title, read by
<code>Template:prevnext/label</code> — the same lookup, and for the same reason, that the
bottom-of-page navigation uses: a label written into the call would be a second copy of a title
that is already translated once, and one nothing checks.

== Why the layout is not in the page ==

Every band of the page is a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> outside the translate tags, so no layout
markup reaches a translator: a unit is a sentence, and moving a band or changing a card's border
is not a change to any sentence. That is the same rule <code>Template:prevnext/doc</code> sets
out.

== What the bands are for ==

The landing page carries what a reader who has just arrived needs, and sends the rest to the
documentation. In order: a tagline and the two buttons; the rule the tool follows, then the
proof of it; four things {{SITENAME}} can do at a line apiece; where it fits and what it is
instead of; one more button; the fine print. Everything else was moved or cut — the list of
what a static export cannot do is an appendix now, on
[[Special:MyLanguage/Limitations|Limitations]], because a landing page that answers "should I
use this" with a list of what is missing has answered the wrong question first.

The bands are short on purpose and the page is long on purpose: a reader scrolls, and would
rather scroll past four lines than read four paragraphs.

== One framed object ==

The transcript under "How it works" is the only filled, bordered box on the page, and that is
the design rather than an accident of it. What you type is a transcript you authored and can
copy; what comes out is a directory. Writing the output as a second
<code>&lt;syntaxhighlight&gt;</code> made the band read as two code blocks that happened to be
adjacent, which is what it was: <code>lang="text"</code> produces a <code>.mw-highlight</code>
box with no tokens in it, so no styling of the wrapper could have said anything else. The tree
is ordinary markup drawn with borders on zero-size boxes, sitting on the page ground.

The cards lost their boxes for the same reason. Four boxes each holding a bold line and one
sentence are boxes that are mostly padding, and they were made of the same light grey bordered
material as the demo, so the page's proof and its list of points were the same object twice.

The panes are stacked rather than side by side. The one line that matters, the
<code>echo</code>, is 41 characters, and half of 54rem does not hold that at every skin's code
font size — it broke a single command across two lines, where it reads as two commands.

== Colours ==

The stylesheet reads the skin's Codex custom properties and defines none. TemplateStyles drops a
custom-property declaration, so a token cannot be made here; it can only be read. Tokens are
defined in <code>MediaWiki:Common.css</code>, which is not sanitised. All three skins redefine
the Codex set for night mode, so reading the tokens is what makes the page follow the reader's
theme without knowing which one it is.

Every shape the page draws — the direction cue above "You get", the tree's trunk and its ticks
— comes out of <code>border-*-color</code> or <code>background-color</code>, both of which are
colour-typed and so may read a token. A <code>var()</code> inside <code>linear-gradient()</code>
cannot be type-checked, so the whole declaration is dropped, silently. That is the trap here.

The filled button is the exception, and it is why <code>MediaWiki:Common.css</code> carries a
<code>--wikven-cta-*</code> pair scoped to <code>.rootpage-index</code>. A button brings its own
background, so it cannot read <code>--color-progressive</code> and be legible: night mode moves
that token to a pale teal that white text sits on at 2.07:1, and the brand teal it replaces sits
on the night ground at 3.2:1, so a button pinned to either one stops being the loudest thing on
the page in half the sessions. The pair inverts instead — dark teal and white by day, light teal
and near-black by night — which measures 5.76:1 and 8.93:1, for the text and for the button
against the ground alike. The scope is the root-page class rather than the page class because the
body of <code>index/ko</code> is <code>page-index_ko</code>, and only <code>rootpage-index</code>
catches the translations too.

== Sizes and lengths ==

Two measures and no more: <code>34rem</code> for anything meant to be read in sentences, and
<code>54rem</code> for the two objects that are looked at rather than read, the demo and the
cards. Five different right edges down one page reads as decisions that accumulated rather than
as a layout.

Lengths come from 0.5 / 1 / 1.5 / 2 / 2.5 / 3.5 / 4 rem and nowhere else. A length cannot be
tokenised here — TemplateStyles drops the declaration — so the scale is kept by hand, and the
next editor inherits the rule from the stylesheet's own header rather than inheriting the values.

The card grid is capped at <code>54rem</code> for a reason worth keeping: four equal items in an
uncapped <code><nowiki>repeat( auto-fit, minmax() )</nowiki></code> orphan the fourth onto a row
of its own at every width that fits three, and which widths those are depends on the skin's root
font size. Three tracks of 18rem with two 1.5rem gaps is 57rem, so under a 54rem cap three cannot
happen — two up on a column, one up on a phone, and both numbers in rem so they scale together.

Section headings are sentence case at 1.375rem. They were a tracked-out caps label, which is a
device for an eyebrow above a headline and was here doing a heading's whole job in the page's
quietest type — and it only ever worked in Latin script, since <code>text-transform</code> does
nothing to Hangul while the letter-spacing still loosens it.
